      <div type="clause" n="6.3">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Ine">Ine</ref> 6.3</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="92" ed="#liebermann"/>Gif<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3-3"/> ðonne on gafolgeldan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4-3"/> huse oððe on gebures gefeohte, CXX<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.star"/> scill. to wite geselle<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.5-2"/> ⁊ þam gebure VI<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.6-2"/> scill.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-bu"><pb n="92" ed="#liebermann"/>Gif mon on . . folgildan . . res gefeohte, CXX scill' to . . . ⁊ þam bure VI scill'.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-h"><pb n="92" ed="#liebermann"/>Gif ðonne on gafolgildon<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.2-2"/> huse oððe on gebures gefeohte, CXX scll' to wite gesylle<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3-2"/> ⁊ þæm<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4-2"/> gebure VI scll'.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-b-no-prol"><pb n="93" ed="#liebermann"/>Gyf ðonne on gafolgylden hu[se<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.1"/>] oððe on gebures gefeohte, hundtwelftig scill' to wite gesylle ⁊ ðæm gebures<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.2"/> syx scill'.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="93" ed="#liebermann"/>Si autem in gauelgilda<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3"/> (id est gablum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4"/> reddentis<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.5"/>) domo<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.6"/> fiat uel in gebures, XXX sol.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.7"/> culpa iudicetur, et ipsi geburo<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.8"/> VI<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.9"/> solid.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="38" ed="#attenborough"/>Gif ðonne<ptr type="editorNote" n="7" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.7"/> on gafolgeldan huse oððe on gebures gefeohte, CXX<ptr type="editorNote" n="8" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.8"/> scill. to wite geselle ⁊ þam gebure<ptr type="editorNote" n="9" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.9"/> VI scill.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="6.3" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="374" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>Gif ðonne on gefolgeldan huse oððe on gebures gefeohte, CXX sciƚƚ to wite geselle ⁊ þæm gebure vi sciƚƚ.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="93" ed="#liebermann"/>Wenn [er] aber im Hause eines Abgabenpflichtigen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.de.1"/> oder in [dem] eines Bauern<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.de.2"/> ficht, zahle er [dem König] 120 [30<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.lnote.de.3"/>] Schill. zur Strafe und dem Bauern 6 Schill.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">But if [he] fights in the house of a rent-paying man or in [that] of a peasant, let him pay [the king] 120 [30] shillings as a fine and the peasant 6 shillings.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="39" ed="#attenborough"/>If, however, he fights in the house of a taxpayer<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.n.1"/> or of a <hi rend="italic">gebur<ptr type="editorNote" n="2" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.n.2"/></hi>, he shall pay 120 shillings<ptr type="editorNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.n.3"/> as a fine, and 6 shillings to the <hi rend="italic">gebur<ptr type="editorNote" n="4" target="#Ine_6-3.attnote.n.4"/></hi>.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><ptr type="editorNote" n="5" target="#Ine_6-3.whinote.n.5"/><pb n="400" ed="#whitelock"/>If then he fights in the house of a rent-payer<ptr type="editorNote" n="3" target="#Ine_6-3.whinote.n.3"/> or a <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi>,<ptr type="editorNote" n="4" target="#Ine_6-3.whinote.n.4"/> he is to pay 120 shillings as a fine and six shillings to the gebur.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="375" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>If he should fight in the house of a renter or a farmer, let him pay 120 shillings as a fine and 6 shillings to the farmer.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="375"><label>cf.</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_6-5">Ine 6.5</ref> <quote>Note that §6.5 set a fine of 30 shillings for an altercation in words rather than blows</quote></note>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#wormald-mel" n="279"><ref target="clause:Af_39">Af 39–39.2</ref></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">se <hi rend="italic">später</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">se later</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">bessere</hi> gebure</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">read gebure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">domo <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Co Or</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">domo added by Co Or</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> gauel <hi rend="italic">Co;</hi> gauel <hi rend="italic">Or</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">altered to gauel Co; gauel Or</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so Lond;</hi> -enti <hi rend="italic">übrige;</hi> -endi <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">thus Lond; -enti the others; -endi T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">pugnatio vicina <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Or</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">pugnatio vicina added by Or</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">solidos <hi rend="italic">Lond;</hi> -dorum <hi rend="italic">R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">solidos Lond; -dorum R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.8" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ieb. <hi rend="italic">T;</hi> tyeb. <hi rend="italic">K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ieb. T; tyeb. K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.9" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">93</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">VII <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">VII Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> -dan</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">altered to -dan</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3-2" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">y <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">y on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4-2" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">æ <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">æ on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.3-3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">he <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">he added by Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.4-3" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">gefolgylden hús <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">gefolgylden hús Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.star" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">bessere</hi> XXX <hi rend="italic">mit Ld Quadr.</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">read XXX with Ld Quadr.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.5-2" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">gesylle ⁊ ðæm <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">gesylle ⁊ ðæm Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.oe.6-2" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">92</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">syx <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">syx Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">69</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Bauer 4c—e (Schutz 15. a, wozu als Beleg für die 30 Pf.-Summe als gemeinfreien Schutz auch hinzuweisen war auf <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> S. 374 n. 7c): ein persönlich Freier, der Bodenzins dem Grundeigentümer schuldet von dem Lande, das er unter ihm, nicht zu echtem Eigen, besitzt</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Bauer 4c—e (Schutz 15. a, where, as evidence for the sum of 30 pence as the protection of the common freeman, reference should also have been made to <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> p. 374 n. 7c): a personally free man who owes ground rent to the landowner for the land that he holds under him, not in full ownership</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">69</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Statt seiner steht <hi rend="italic">ceorl</hi> Af 39</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">In its place <hi rend="italic">ceorl</hi> stands in Af 39</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_6-3.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">69</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Der Archetyp las 120. Im 11. Jh. besserte ein Schreiber lq ‘30’, in Ines Sinne, wie Price und Schmid annehmen. Denn Ine konnte nicht höhere Fechtstrafe fordern von dem, der bei seiner Bluttat nur niederen od. keinen (6, 4) Hausfrieden als von dem, der jene hohen Frieden verletzte; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> blutig fechten 3a. Sollte hier Ælfred etwa Ines Strafgeldfixum im Sinne späteren ‘Ungehorsams’ zugunsten der Krone erhöht haben?</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The archetype read 120. In the 11th cent. a scribe, lq, corrected this to ‘30’, in accordance with Ine’s intention, as Price and Schmid assume. For Ine could not demand a higher fine for fighting from a man who in his act of bloodshed broke only a lower house-peace, or none at all (6, 4), than from one who broke those high peaces; <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> blutig fechten 3a. Could Ælfred perhaps have raised Ine’s fixed fine here in favour of the Crown, in the sense of the later ‘disobedience’?</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.7" n="7" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">38</biblScope></bibl><label>ðonne</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">Gif mon</hi>. Bu.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.8" n="8" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">38</biblScope></bibl><label>cxx</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">xxx sol</hi>. Quad.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.fn.9" n="9" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">38</biblScope></bibl><label>gebure</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">bure</hi>. Bu.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">183</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">One of the commonest Latin terms for ‘hide’ is <hi rend="italic">tributarius</hi>, which strictly speaking appears to be a translation of <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda.</hi> It would seem, therefore, that a <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> was, whether in Ine’s time or at some time not long previously, a peasant who held a hide of land. If we compare cap. 23 § 3 with cap. 32 we are brought to the same conclusion. The word <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> does not occur elsewhere in the laws.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">184</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">For the <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi> of later times see <hi rend="italic">Rect.</hi> cap. 4. In Domesday Book the word is translated by <hi rend="italic">colibertus.</hi> In Ine’s time it may have had a wider significance. The presumption is that the terms <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> and <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi> together comprise the whole peasant population, and that, in contradistinction to the former term, <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi> denotes one who did not possess a holding of his own (see Chadwick, <hi rend="italic">Anglo-Saxon Institutions</hi>, p. 87 ff.).</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.n.3" n="3" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">184</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Liebermann adds, ‘to the king,’ both here and in § 4 and § 5.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.attnote.n.4" n="4" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">184</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> is presumably to be included with the <hi rend="italic">gebur.</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.whinote.n.3" n="3" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">400</biblScope></bibl><label>rent-payer</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The term used is <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi>. On its meaning, <hi rend="italic">see</hi> next note.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.whinote.n.4" n="4" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">400</biblScope></bibl><label>gebur</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">The <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi> is called by Sir Frank Stenton “a free, but economically dependent peasant”. He received his land and stock from a lord and rendered agricultural services and other dues. If Ine’s law were meant to cover all classes of free peasant in this chapter, <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> would have to be applied to the peasant who farmed land of his own; but, though Alfred 39 speaks simply of <hi rend="italic">ceorl</hi> in this connection, it is not safe to assume that this was Ine’s meaning. Alfred may be improving on Ine’s ruling. Ine’s list here of compensations for fighting in houses is surely not meant to be exhaustive; the <hi rend="italic">gesithcund</hi> classes are omitted, so it would not be odd if the peasant farming his own land received no mention. The law is probably confined to cases about which there could be uncertainty, or where it was desired to make an alteration. If we assume this, we may take the <hi rend="italic">gafolgelda</hi> to be, as the word suggests, one who holds land from a lord at a rent; and both he and the <hi rend="italic">gebur</hi> can be included in the phrase in Alfred’s treaty with Guthrum, “the <hi rend="italic">ceorl</hi> who occupies <hi rend="italic">gafolland</hi>” (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> p. 416).</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_6-3.whinote.n.5" n="5" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">400</biblScope></bibl><label>120</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Nowell’s transcript of Otho B. xi reads XXX, as does <hi rend="italic">Quadripartitus</hi>. This seems a more likely figure, but both these sources have CXX in the next chapter.</seg></note>
